der book & pipeline","body":"Rs.15Cr from Levi’s San Francisco division; additional sampling approved for Agilitas; Primark and M&S audits in progress (expected onboarding in 3-4 months)","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"guidance":{"title":"Guidance","body":"Revenue growth: targeting Rs.700Cr total revenue post-600-machine rollout; Margin: expected to improve post-2027 automation efficiency gains; Order inflow: new audits underway with Primark, Marks & Spencer; Capex/commissioning: Rs.60Cr in FY26-27; full 600-machine rollout over 3 years (2027 end)","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"growth_optionality":{"title":"Growth verticals / optionality","body":"Expansion into European markets (UK, Germany); Potential entry into mass-market brands (Primark, M&S); Leveraging 3D design tech to attract new global clients; Long-term revenue upside to Rs.700Cr post-capex completion","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"margins":{"title":"Margins & cost dynamics","body":"Q2 EBITDA margin dipped to 8.88% due to 8% YoY employee benefit cost increase (wages + benefits) — temporary labor inflation from retention needs amid market uncertainty; Raw material (cotton/yarn) stable; Margin recovery expected post-automation efficiency gains (labor + waste reduction) from 2027","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"capital_allocation":{"title":"Capital allocation","body":"100% focused on capex for smart knitting machines; No dividends or buybacks mentioned; No new debt raised; Supplier financing eliminates need for external capital; Working capital managed efficiently (positive OCF)","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"management_tone":{"title":"Management quality / tone","body":"Confident and strategic — framing US trade disruption as temporary, emphasizing automation as non-negotiable future, highlighting supplier financing as a strength, projecting Rs.700Cr revenue target with clear roadmap; tone is optimistic but grounded in operational discipline","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"risks":{"title":"Risks & watch-items","body":"US tariff uncertainty impacting demand visibility; Slow adoption in new European markets; Competitive pressure from displaced Chinese/other Indian suppliers; Execution risk in 3-year machine rollout; Labor cost inflation if wage hikes persist beyond 2026","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"non_obvious":{"title":"Non-obvious takeaways","body":"The Rs.60Cr capex is not a cash outflow — it’s supplier-financed machinery; the real capital risk is borne by Ningbo, not Active Clothing; this is a rare case of capex being funded by equipment vendor — a structural advantage","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"},"challenges":{"title":"Challenges → beneficiaries & losers","body":"| Challenge | Beneficiary/Loser | Dir | Conviction | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| American market disruption due to tariffs | Other Indian apparel manufacturers | loser | High | Active is less exposed than peers (only 25% export to US), and is using the disruption to build automation moat — competitors without capex will lose market share |\n| Rising labor costs and retention pressure | Active Clothing Co Limited | winner | Medium | Labor cost inflation is temporary and being offset by automation — company is investing in retention to secure skilled workforce for future smart factory |","src":"concall","as_of":"Q2FY26"}}}